# Page titles and descriptions (frontmatter)

Every page's `title` and `description` frontmatter is what users see in search
results, browser tabs, and link previews. A generic title ("Overview", "Tips")
or a vague description means users cannot tell what the page covers before
clicking. Write them for someone scanning a list of results.

These rules are a standing contract for **every page you create or edit**, not
only for dedicated SEO passes. A new page ships with its final title and
description in every locale it is published in. A body, image, workflow or i18n
edit does not reopen frontmatter that already follows these rules: see
[Do not retouch working frontmatter](#do-not-retouch-working-frontmatter).

## Title rules

- Lead with the specific subject: the model, tool, or feature name. Generic
  words like Overview, Tips, FAQ, or Getting Started are not enough on their
  own. Pair them with the subject: "Custom node development tips",
  "Get started with Comfy CLI".
- When a model has an official product name, lead with it and keep that official
  spelling in every locale: "Wan Animate 2: Motion Transfer".
- On a model or tutorial page the model name comes first. Never open such a
  title with `ComfyUI`.
- Say what the page helps the user do when the name alone does not.
- Keep the EN title at or under about 60 characters, with 65 as the ceiling.
  Length is a guideline, not a gate. If a title runs long, cut filler words
  ("and", "Native", "Official") before cutting a capability, never pad a title
  to reach a number, and never drop a real capability fact to fit one.
- Never leave the title identical to the sidebar title when the sidebar is a
  bare generic word; the title can be longer and more specific.
- Localize titles for zh/ja/ko: translate the capability and keep the official
  product name untranslated.

### Title formulas

| Locale | Model or tutorial page | Utility / multi-model page |
|---|---|---|
| EN | `{Model} in ComfyUI: {capability}` | `{Topic} in ComfyUI: {scope}`, or the already shipped `ComfyUI {Topic}` form |
| zh | `{Model}：ComfyUI {capability}` (fullwidth `：`) | `{主题}：ComfyUI {范围}` |
| ja | `{Model}: ComfyUI {capability}` (ASCII `:`) | `{トピック}: ComfyUI {範囲}` |
| ko | `{Model}: ComfyUI {capability}` (ASCII `:`) | `{주제}: ComfyUI {범위}` |

- `Wan2.2 Fun Control in ComfyUI: Video Generation from a Control Video`
- zh `Wan2.2 Fun Control：ComfyUI 基于控制视频的视频生成`
- ja `Wan2.2 Fun Control: ComfyUI 制御動画からの動画生成`
- Utility page: `Video Upscaling in ComfyUI: Models and Use Cases`
- Interface and development pages that already ship as `ComfyUI <Topic>`
  (`ComfyUI Settings Overview`) keep that shape.

A title that already leads with the model name and names the capability
(`Wan Animate 2: Motion Transfer`) follows this contract. Do not insert
`in ComfyUI` into it just to match the shape of the formula. The formulas apply
to new pages and to titles being retouched for one of the reasons in
[Do not retouch working frontmatter](#do-not-retouch-working-frontmatter).

`sidebarTitle` is the short navigation label. Leave it alone unless the
navigation itself is wrong, and never change it as part of an SEO pass.

## Description rules

- Every page must have one. A missing description makes search engines invent
  their own snippet from random page text.
- 40 to 160 characters. Under 40 is too thin to be useful; over 160 gets cut
  off in search results. Aim for 120 to 155.
- On a model page, lead with the action, the model, and the modality, then name
  one to three concrete deliverables: variants, duration limits, or the named
  workflows the page shows. On a utility, multi-model, or reference page, lead
  with the topic and the scope the page covers instead. Do not invent a model
  or a modality the page does not have.
- Stop when the facts are complete. Do not stretch a finished description with
  "and setup steps" or "and more" just to reach a character target.
- Describe what the user will find and do on the page, covering the page's
  actual scope (for a model page: what generation tasks it handles, what
  workflows are shown).
- Do not copy the title into the description. The description adds information
  the title does not already say.
- Put the key facts in the first 120 characters: search results may truncate
  mid-sentence on narrow screens.
- No marketing superlatives ("best", "ultimate", "powerful"). State facts.
- Quote the value when it contains a colon followed by a space, or any other
  YAML-special sequence. Unquoted `key: value` text inside a description
  breaks frontmatter parsing.
- Localize descriptions for zh/ja/ko: they must convey the same scope, not be
  a character-for-character translation. Localized dates format automatically.

## Banned title and description patterns

Titles whose only descriptor is a template placeholder, and descriptions that
never name the deliverable:

| Banned | Replace with |
|---|---|
| `{Model} ComfyUI Workflow Example` / `... Examples` | `{Model} in ComfyUI: {capability}` |
| `... Official Example`, `... Official Native Workflow Example` | the capabilities the page covers |
| `... Usage Example`, `... Native Example`, `... Tutorial`, `... Guide` | the capability, without the filler noun |
| Bare official name with no capability (`Kandinsky 5.0`) | `{Model} in ComfyUI: {capability}` |
| `This guide/article/example shows how to ...` | the action, the model, and what the reader gets |
| `... and full setup steps` padding on an already complete description | the description without the padding |

Do not invent capabilities the page does not cover. Facts must match the page
body: modality, duration limits, variants, and model family.

## Do not retouch working frontmatter

Titles and descriptions get rewritten far more often than they need to be.
Every frontmatter edit costs a review round, re-runs CI, and on a localized
page reopens translation hashes and parity checks. Change a title or
description only when at least one of these is true:

1. The title matches a banned pattern above.
2. The description opens with `This guide/article/example ...`, never names what
   the reader gets, or is missing entirely.
3. The frontmatter contradicts the page body (wrong modality, wrong duration,
   wrong model family, or a variant the page does not cover).
4. The EN title runs past about 65 characters and the capability is cut off.
5. A localized title drops the official model name, or leads with `ComfyUI` on a
   model page.

Otherwise leave it. A title that already follows the formula is not improved by
a second pass, and re-editing it does not buy ranking; it buys review time.

Process rules that follow from this:

- One pass per page, batched by family: every page of one model, one directory,
  or one template-generated set goes in the same commit, together with the
  zh/ja/ko counterparts that already exist. A missing translation is not part
  of a frontmatter pass.
- Do not append a few pages a day to a long-lived SEO PR. Each drip re-runs
  review and CI on the whole branch.
- Do not mix frontmatter rewrites into a content, image, or i18n PR, and do not
  mix content edits into a frontmatter PR.

## Anti-patterns and fixes

| Bad | Why | Better |
|---|---|---|
| `title: Overview` | Says nothing about which overview | `title: "ComfyUI Manager Configuration"` |
| `title: Tips` | Unfindable in search | `title: "Custom Node Development Tips"` |
| `title: ComfyUI Flux.2 Dev Example` | Template placeholder, no capability | `title: "FLUX.2 Dev in ComfyUI: Text-to-Image and Product Mockups"` |
| `description: Using ComfyUI as a Developer` | 5 words, tells the user almost nothing | `description: "Build on ComfyUI with official Python and TypeScript SDKs, run it as a server, call Cloud APIs, or connect AI agents via MCP."` |
| description duplicating the title word for word | Wastes the snippet slot | Cover scope the title leaves out |
| `description: The best guide to everything ComfyUI` | Superlatives + overbroad scope | List what the page actually covers |

## PR hygiene for a frontmatter pass

- Frontmatter only, plus unavoidable parity fixes (for example a code-fence
  literal that must stay identical to English).
- One table in the PR body: page, old title, new title, why.
- Run the structure-parity and anchor checks. No body prose drive-bys.

## Examples

Good model page:

```yaml
title: "Wan Animate 2: Motion Transfer"
description: "Animate a still character using a driving video with Wan Animate 2, transferring motion directly from video frames without pose extraction or skeleton preprocessing"
```

Good localized model page:

```yaml
title: "Kandinsky 5.0：ComfyUI 文生视频与图生视频"
```

Good reference page:

```yaml
title: "Custom Node Development Tips"
description: "Practical tips for developing custom nodes for ComfyUI, including frontend/backend separation, packaging, and common API pitfalls"
```
